4, "start": 172.85999999999999, "end": 178.18, "text": " up emerging from the cosmic dark ages. The very first stars were beginning to burn away the fog", "tokens": [50931, 493, 14989, 490, 264, 27614, 2877, 12357, 13, 440, 588, 700, 6105, 645, 2863, 281, 5064, 1314, 264, 13648, 51197], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06109180147685702, "compression_ratio": 1.5386996904024768, "no_speech_prob": 1.5491473121659327e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 16154, "start": 178.18, "end": 184.2, "text": " of neutral hydrogen. So it's as primitive as it gets. Exactly. And what the JWST found when it looked", "tokens": [51197, 295, 10598, 12697, 13, 407, 309, 311, 382, 28540, 382, 309, 2170, 13, 7587, 13, 400, 437, 264, 49885, 6840, 1352, 562, 309, 2956, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06109180147685702, "compression_ratio": 1.5386996904024768, "no_speech_prob": 1.5491473121659327e-12}, {"id": 32, "seek": 16154, "start": 184.2, "end": 191.07999999999998, "text": " at LAP-1b broke a lot of foundational assumptions. Yeah the sources say its oxygen abundance is staggeringly", "tokens": [51498, 412, 441, 4715, 12, 16, 65, 6902, 257, 688, 295, 32195, 17695, 13, 865, 264, 7139, 584, 1080, 9169, 23391, 307, 29656, 12163, 51842], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06109180147685702, "compression_ratio": 1.5386996904024768, "no_speech_prob": 1.5491473121659327e-12}, {"id": 33, "seek": 19108, "start": 191.08, "end": 198.48000000000002, "text": " low. Specifically 4.2 plus or minus 1.8 times 10 to the negative third solar. Which sounds like a", "tokens": [50365, 2295, 13, 26058, 1017, 13, 17, 1804, 420, 3175, 502, 13, 23, 1413, 1266, 281, 264, 3671, 2636, 7936, 13, 3013, 3263, 411, 257, 50735], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08016802971823174, "compression_ratio": 1.5934426229508196, "no_speech_prob": 1.8179316559063796e-12}, {"id": 34, "seek": 19108, "start": 198.48000000000002, "end": 203.74, "text": " mouthful. But to put that in perspective that is a fraction of a single percent of our own sun's", "tokens": [50735, 4525, 906, 13, 583, 281, 829, 300, 294, 4585, 300, 307, 257, 14135, 295, 257, 2167, 3043, 295, 527, 1065, 3295, 311, 50998], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08016802971823174, "compression_ratio": 1.5934426229508196, "no_speech_prob": 1.8179316559063796e-12}, {"id": 35, "seek": 19108, "start": 203.74, "end": 209.46, "text": " metallicity. Yeah it completely shatters the previously assumed 2 percent metallicity floor", "tokens": [50998, 25759, 507, 13, 865, 309, 2584, 402, 37690, 264, 8046, 15895, 568, 3043, 25759, 507, 4123, 51284], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08016802971823174, "compression_ratio": 1.5934426229508196, "no_speech_prob": 1.8179316559063796e-12}, {"id": 36, "seek": 19108, "start": 209.46, "end": 214.18, "text": " for these early objects. We thought things had to have at least a certain amount of heavy elements", "tokens": [51284, 337, 613, 2440, 6565, 13, 492, 1194, 721, 632, 281, 362, 412, 1935, 257, 1629, 2372, 295, 4676, 4959, 51520], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08016802971823174, "compression_ratio": 1.5934426229508196, "no_speech_prob": 1.8179316559063796e-12}, {"id": 37, "seek": 19108, "start": 214.18, "end": 220.02, "text": " by that point. Right. Astronomers call anything heavier than hydrogen and helium a metal. So finding", "tokens": [51520, 538, 300, 935, 13, 1779, 13, 36819, 298, 433, 818, 1340, 18279, 813, 12697, 293, 40175, 257, 5760, 13, 407, 5006, 51812], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08016802971823174, "compression_ratio": 1.5934426229508196, "no_speech_prob": 1.8179316559063796e-12}, {"id": 38, "seek": 22002, "start": 220.02, "end": 224.54000000000002, "text": " something with almost zero metals is a huge deal. And the mass of this thing is even weirder.", "tokens": [50365, 746, 365, 1920, 4018, 22548, 307, 257, 2603, 2028, 13, 400, 264, 2758, 295, 341, 551, 307, 754, 321, 347, 1068, 13, 50591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056457290649414064, "compression_ratio": 1.592, "no_speech_prob": 2.116596611520527e-12}, {"id": 39, "seek": 22002, "start": 225.04000000000002, "end": 232.44, "text": " The stellar mass-like, the actual glowing stars we can account for, is under 3,300 solar masses. Which", "tokens": [50616, 440, 42333, 2758, 12, 4092, 11, 264, 3539, 27064, 6105, 321, 393, 2696, 337, 11, 307, 833, 805, 11, 12566, 7936, 23935, 13, 3013, 50986], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056457290649414064, "compression_ratio": 1.592, "no_speech_prob": 2.116596611520527e-12}, {"id": 40, "seek": 22002, "start": 232.44, "end": 238.0, "text": " is tiny. But its dynamical mass, how it's actually behaving gravitationally and pulling on the space", "tokens": [50986, 307, 5870, 13, 583, 1080, 5999, 804, 2758, 11, 577, 309, 311, 767, 35263, 7427, 4614, 379, 293, 8407, 322, 264, 1901, 51264], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056457290649414064, "compression_ratio": 1.592, "no_speech_prob": 2.116596611520527e-12}, {"id": 41, "seek": 22002, "start": 238.0, "end": 244.72, "text": " around it, is around 10 million solar masses. That disparity is massive. I mean the luminous matter,", "tokens": [51264, 926, 309, 11, 307, 926, 1266, 2459, 7936, 23935, 13, 663, 47415, 307, 5994, 13, 286, 914, 264, 32476, 563, 1871, 11, 51600], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056457290649414064, "compression_ratio": 1.592, "no_speech_prob": 2.116596611520527e-12}, {"id": 42, "seek": 24472, "start": 244.72, "end": 250.35999999999999, "text": " those 3,300 solar masses of stars make up a tiny fraction of the system. Less than one percent.", "tokens": [50365, 729, 805, 11, 12566, 7936, 23935, 295, 6105, 652, 493, 257, 5870, 14135, 295, 264, 1185, 13, 18649, 813, 472, 3043, 13, 50647], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09056352887834822, "compression_ratio": 1.498069498069498, "no_speech_prob": 1.5853084903844072e-12}, {"id": 43, "seek": 24472, "start": 250.35999999999999, "end": 255.24, "text": " So what's the rest of it? Well the rest is an unobserved organizing structure. What standard", "tokens": [50647, 407, 437, 311, 264, 1472, 295, 309, 30, 1042, 264, 1472, 307, 364, 8526, 929, 6913, 17608, 3877, 13, 708, 3832, 50891], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09056352887834822, "compression_ratio": 1.498069498069498, "no_speech_prob": 1.5853084903844072e-12}, {"id": 44, "seek": 24472, "start": 255.24, "end": 261.48, "text": " cosmology would usually just wave away is a dark matter halo. But what makes LAP-1b so critical", "tokens": [50891, 22207, 1793, 576, 2673, 445, 5772, 1314, 307, 257, 2877, 1871, 46268, 13, 583, 437, 1669, 441, 4715, 12, 16, 65, 370, 4924, 51203], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09056352887834822, "compression_ratio": 1.498069498069498, "no_speech_prob": 1.5853084903844072e-12}, {"id": 45, "seek": 24472, "start": 261.48, "end": 269.7, "text": " for Fliction's RSVP theory isn't just that it's old or small or heavy in the dark. It's that it is what", "tokens": [51203, 337, 3235, 4105, 311, 25855, 53, 47, 5261, 1943, 380, 445, 300, 309, 311, 1331, 420, 1359, 420, 4676, 294, 264, 2877, 13, 467, 311, 300, 309, 307, 437, 51614], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09056352887834822, "compression_ratio": 1.498069498069498, "no_speech_prob": 1.5853084903844072e-12}, {"id": 46, "seek": 26970, "start": 269.7, "end": 275.84, "text": " the monographs call a least overwritten system. A least overwritten system. Okay. To understand", "tokens": [50365, 264, 1108, 3108, 82, 818, 257, 1935, 670, 26859, 1185, 13, 316, 1935, 670, 26859, 1185, 13, 1033, 13, 1407, 1223, 50672], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.075300741633144, "compression_ratio": 1.6722972972972974, "no_speech_prob": 1.518626695749814e-12}, {"id": 47, "seek": 26970, "start": 275.84, "end": 282.4, "text": " why that matters, we need to completely rethink how we view the chemical evolution of galaxies.", "tokens": [50672, 983, 300, 7001, 11, 321, 643, 281, 2584, 34595, 577, 321, 1910, 264, 7313, 9303, 295, 28755, 13, 51000], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.075300741633144, "compression_ratio": 1.6722972972972974, "no_speech_prob": 1.518626695749814e-12}, {"id": 48, "seek": 26970, "start": 282.62, "end": 286.24, "text": " Because we normally think of it as building up, right? Right. The standard models we teach in", "tokens": [51011, 1436, 321, 5646, 519, 295, 309, 382, 2390, 493, 11, 558, 30, 1779, 13, 440, 3832, 5245, 321, 2924, 294, 51192], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.075300741633144, "compression_ratio": 1.6722972972972974, "no_speech_prob": 1.518626695749814e-12}, {"id": 49, "seek": 26970, "start": 286.24, "end": 292.24, "text": " universities treat galactic history as an evolution. A gradual building up of complexity. You start with", "tokens": [51192, 11779, 2387, 7660, 19892, 2503, 382, 364, 9303, 13, 316, 32890, 2390, 493, 295, 14024, 13, 509, 722, 365, 51492], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.075300741633144, "compression_ratio": 1.6722972972972974, "no_speech_prob": 1.518626695749814e-12}, {"id": 50, "seek": 26970, "start": 292.24, "end": 297.46, "text": " hydrogen and helium. Stars forge heavier elements. They explode. And then the next generation uses those", "tokens": [51492, 12697, 293, 40175, 13, 20957, 38741, 18279, 4959, 13, 814, 21411, 13, 400, 550, 264, 958, 5125, 4960, 729, 51753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.075300741633144, "compression_ratio": 1.6722972972972974, "no_speech_prob": 1.518626695749814e-12}, {"id": 51, "seek": 29746, "start": 297.46, "end": 302.91999999999996, "text": " elements and so on. Exactly. But Fliction argues evolution is the wrong word entirely. Yeah. It is", "tokens": [50365, 4959, 293, 370, 322, 13, 7587, 13, 583, 3235, 4105, 38218, 9303, 307, 264, 2085, 1349, 7696, 13, 865, 13, 467, 307, 50638], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07871216062515501, "compression_ratio": 1.6146496815286624, "no_speech_prob": 1.6228904071297068e-12}, {"id": 52, "seek": 29746, "start": 302.91999999999996, 