at brown", "tokens": [50365, 4276, 11, 309, 445, 4523, 666, 257, 5257, 4133, 6292, 1061, 16032, 13, 316, 2176, 636, 281, 829, 309, 13, 509, 393, 574, 412, 300, 6292, 50603], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0759633719319045, "compression_ratio": 1.5465116279069768, "no_speech_prob": 1.4266240098981253e-12}, {"id": 57, "seek": 32596, "start": 330.71999999999997, "end": 336.52, "text": " water all day, but you can never, ever reconstruct the individual drops that went into it. The history", "tokens": [50603, 1281, 439, 786, 11, 457, 291, 393, 1128, 11, 1562, 31499, 264, 2609, 11438, 300, 1437, 666, 309, 13, 440, 2503, 50893], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0759633719319045, "compression_ratio": 1.5465116279069768, "no_speech_prob": 1.4266240098981253e-12}, {"id": 58, "seek": 32596, "start": 336.52, "end": 345.2, "text": " is gone. So LAP1B is the undisturbed red drop. That is a remarkably precise analogy. And it captures", "tokens": [50893, 307, 2780, 13, 407, 441, 4715, 16, 33, 307, 264, 674, 468, 374, 2883, 2182, 3270, 13, 663, 307, 257, 37381, 13600, 21663, 13, 400, 309, 27986, 51327], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0759633719319045, "compression_ratio": 1.5465116279069768, "no_speech_prob": 1.4266240098981253e-12}, {"id": 59, "seek": 32596, "start": 345.2, "end": 350.35999999999996, "text": " the tragedy of astronomical observation, really. Every successive generation of star formation,", "tokens": [51327, 264, 18563, 295, 49035, 14816, 11, 534, 13, 2048, 48043, 5125, 295, 3543, 11723, 11, 51585], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0759633719319045, "compression_ratio": 1.5465116279069768, "no_speech_prob": 1.4266240098981253e-12}, {"id": 60, "seek": 35036, "start": 350.36, "end": 356.44, "text": " every supernova explosion. Every time galaxies collide. Exactly. Every time two galaxies merge, they stir the", "tokens": [50365, 633, 1687, 39486, 15673, 13, 2048, 565, 28755, 49093, 13, 7587, 13, 2048, 565, 732, 28755, 22183, 11, 436, 8946, 264, 50669], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05567772833855598, "compression_ratio": 1.6706827309236947, "no_speech_prob": 1.4957705213314298e-12}, {"id": 61, "seek": 35036, "start": 356.44, "end": 360.88, "text": " cosmic pot, they inject new metals, they dilute the old ones, and they spread everything around through", "tokens": [50669, 27614, 1847, 11, 436, 10711, 777, 22548, 11, 436, 11504, 1169, 264, 1331, 2306, 11, 293, 436, 3974, 1203, 926, 807, 50891], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05567772833855598, "compression_ratio": 1.6706827309236947, "no_speech_prob": 1.4957705213314298e-12}, {"id": 62, "seek": 35036, "start": 360.88, "end": 366.62, "text": " turbulence. So the history gets lost. The specific distinct history of earlier events gets completely", "tokens": [50891, 48612, 13, 407, 264, 2503, 2170, 2731, 13, 440, 2685, 10644, 2503, 295, 3071, 3931, 2170, 2584, 51178], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05567772833855598, "compression_ratio": 1.6706827309236947, "no_speech_prob": 1.4957705213314298e-12}, {"id": 63, "seek": 35036, "start": 366.62, "end": 372.96000000000004, "text": " overwritten. So a mature galaxy like our own Milky Way is basically that glass of murky brown water.", "tokens": [51178, 670, 26859, 13, 407, 257, 14442, 17639, 411, 527, 1065, 38465, 9558, 307, 1936, 300, 4276, 295, 5257, 4133, 6292, 1281, 13, 51495], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05567772833855598, "compression_ratio": 1.6706827309236947, "no_speech_prob": 1.4957705213314298e-12}, {"id": 64, "seek": 37296, "start": 372.96, "end": 379.18, "text": " Precisely. The Milky Way has high chemical complexity. It has all the elements on the periodic table in", "tokens": [50365, 48746, 736, 13, 440, 38465, 9558, 575, 1090, 7313, 14024, 13, 467, 575, 439, 264, 4959, 322, 264, 27790, 3199, 294, 50676], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0769385424527255, "compression_ratio": 1.6568627450980393, "no_speech_prob": 1.530861917266313e-12}, {"id": 65, "seek": 37296, "start": 379.18, "end": 384.32, "text": " abundance, but it has incredibly low historical resolution. Oh, I like that term, historical", "tokens": [50676, 23391, 11, 457, 309, 575, 6252, 2295, 8584, 8669, 13, 876, 11, 286, 411, 300, 1433, 11, 8584, 50933], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0769385424527255, "compression_ratio": 1.6568627450980393, "no_speech_prob": 1.530861917266313e-12}, {"id": 66, "seek": 37296, "start": 384.32, "end": 390.46, "text": " resolution. You cannot look at the current chemical state of our solar neighborhood and untangle the", "tokens": [50933, 8669, 13, 509, 2644, 574, 412, 264, 2190, 7313, 1785, 295, 527, 7936, 7630, 293, 1701, 7846, 264, 51240], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0769385424527255, "compression_ratio": 1.6568627450980393, "no_speech_prob": 1.530861917266313e-12}, {"id": 67, "seek": 37296, "start": 390.46, "end": 397.32, "text": " specific sequence of individual nucleosynthetic episodes. You can't see the specific ancient supernovae", "tokens": [51240, 2685, 8310, 295, 2609, 14962, 329, 18656, 3532, 9313, 13, 509, 393, 380, 536, 264, 2685, 7832, 1687, 39486, 68, 51583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0769385424527255, "compression_ratio": 1.6568627450980393, "no_speech_prob": 1.530861917266313e-12}, {"id": 68, "seek": 37296, "start": 397.32, "end": 402.38, "text": " that created it. Because they're all mixed together. Right. So the principle of least overwritten systems", "tokens": [51583, 300, 2942, 309, 13, 1436, 436, 434, 439, 7467, 1214, 13, 1779, 13, 407, 264, 8665, 295, 1935, 670, 26859, 3652, 51836], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0769385424527255, "compression_ratio": 1.6568627450980393, "no_speech_prob": 1.530861917266313e-12}, {"id": 69, "seek": 40238, "start": 402.38, "end": 407.46, "text": " states that information privilege belongs to systems that have undergone the fewest averaging", "tokens": [50365, 4368, 300, 1589, 12122, 12953, 281, 3652, 300, 362, 833, 39743, 264, 1326, 377, 47308, 50619], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10118060416363656, "compression_ratio": 1.5525291828793775, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758169067183527e-12}, {"id": 70, "seek": 40238, "start": 407.46, "end": 413.5, "text": " operations. Which means it's not just about how old something is. No, not at all. Because a young,", "tokens": [50619, 7705, 13, 3013, 1355, 309, 311, 406, 445, 466, 577, 1331, 746, 307, 13, 883, 11, 406, 412, 439, 13, 1436, 257, 2037, 11, 50921], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10118060416363656, "compression_ratio": 1.5525291828793775, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758169067183527e-12}, {"id": 71, "seek": 40238, "start": 413.71999999999997, "end": 420.56, "text": " massive, highly active galaxy might be totally overwritten in just a few million years. While a very old,", "tokens": [50932, 5994, 11, 5405, 4967, 17639, 1062, 312, 3879, 670, 26859, 294, 445, 257, 1326, 2459, 924, 13, 3987, 257, 588, 1331, 11, 51274], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10118060416363656, "compression_ratio": 1.5525291828793775, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758169067183527e-12}, {"id": 72, "seek": 40238, "start": 420.56, "end": 427.94, "text": " but isolated, low mass system preserves its original event log perfectly. And LAP-1b is the ultimate", "tokens": [51274, 457, 14621, 11, 2295, 2758, 1185, 1183, 9054, 1080, 3380, 2280, 3565, 6239, 13, 400, 441, 4715, 12, 16, 65, 307, 264, 9705, 51643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10118060416363656, "compression_ratio": 1.5525291828793775, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758169067183527e-12}, {"id": 73, "seek": 42794, "start": 427.94, "end": 433.96, "text": " preserved event log. It has a very specific, weird signature in its chemistry. It has an elevated", "tokens": [50365, 22242, 2280, 3565, 13, 467, 575, 257, 588, 2685, 11, 3657, 13397, 294, 1080, 12558, 13, 467, 575, 364, 23457, 50666], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07068733815793637, "compression_ratio": 1.627177700348432, "no_speech_prob": 1.410000371668274e-12}, {"id": 74, "seek": 42794, "start": 433.96, "end": 438.04, "text": " carbon to oxygen ratio. Which makes no sense initially. I mean, why would it have high carbon", "tokens": [50666, 5954, 281, 9169, 8509, 13, 3013, 1669, 572, 2020, 9105, 13, 286, 914, 11, 983, 576, 309, 362, 1090, 5954, 50870], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07068733815793637, "compression_ratio": 1.627177700348432, "no_speech_prob": 1.410000371668274e-12}, {"id": 75, "seek": 42794, "start": 438.04, "end": 443.7, "text": " but practically no oxygen? That specific chemical signature brings us to the ghost of faint population", "tokens": [50870, 457, 15667, 572, 9169, 30, 663, 2685, 7313, 13397, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 8359, 295, 21104, 4415, 51153], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07068733815793637, "compression_ratio": 1.627177700348432, "no_speech_prob": 1.410000371668274e-12}, {"id": 76, "seek": 42794, "start": 443.7, "end": 449.15999999999997, "text": " 3's supernovae. Okay. Population 3 stars being the hypothetical very first stars in the universe,", "tokens": [51153, 805, 311, 1687, 39486, 68, 13, 1033, 13, 10215, 2776, 805, 6105, 885, 264, 33053, 588, 700, 6105, 294, 264, 6445, 11, 51426], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07068733815793637, "compression_ratio": 1.627177700348432, "no_speech_prob": 1.410000371668274e-12}, {"id": 77, "seek": 42794, "start": 449.15999999999997, "end": 454.08, "text": " made of pure hydrogen and helium. Exactly. They were massive, short-lived,", "tokens": [51426, 1027, 295, 6075, 12697, 293, 40175, 13, 7587, 13, 814, 645, 5994, 11, 2099, 12, 46554, 11, 51672], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07068733815793637, "compression_ratio": 1.627177700348432, "no_speech_prob": 1.410000371668274e-12}, {"id": 78, "seek": 45408, "start": 454.08, "end": 460.41999999999996, "text": " and when they died, they seeded the universe with the first heavy metals. Through supernovae. Right.", "tokens": [50365, 293, 562, 436, 4539, 11, 436, 8871, 292, 264, 6445, 365, 264, 700, 4676, 22548, 13, 8927, 1687, 39486, 68, 13, 1779, 13, 50682], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.117191873415552, "compression_ratio": 1.5454545454545454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417448776660405e-12}, {"id": 79, "seek": 45408, "start": 460.85999999999996, "end": 467.15999999999997, "text": " Now, in typical highly energetic supernovae that we see today, both carbon and oxygen are blasted", "tokens": [50704, 823, 11, 294, 7476, 5405, 24935, 1687, 39486, 68, 300, 321, 536, 965, 11, 1293, 5954, 293, 9169, 366, 12035, 292, 51019], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.117191873415552, "compression_ratio": 1.5454545454545454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417448776660405e-12}, {"id": 80, "seek": 45408, "start": 467.15999999999997, "end": 472.9, "text": " outward into space with immense force. Both of them are formed in the layers of the star's core.", "tokens": [51019, 26914, 666, 1901, 365, 22920, 3464, 13, 6767, 295, 552, 366, 8693, 294, 264, 7914, 295, 264, 3543, 311, 4965, 13, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.117191873415552, "compression_ratio": 1.5454545454545454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417448776660405e-12}, {"id": 81, "seek": 45408, "start": 473.09999999999997, "end": 479.02, "text": " So they both get spread around. Usually, yes. But Flixion's analysis points to faint pop-thread", "tokens": [51316, 407, 436, 1293, 483, 3974, 926, 13, 11419, 11, 2086, 13, 583, 3235, 970, 313, 311, 5215, 2793, 281, 21104, 1665, 12, 392, 2538, 51612], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.117191873415552, "compression_ratio": 1.5454545454545454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417448776660405e-12}, {"id": 82, "seek": 47902, "start": 479.02, "end": 485.38, "text": " events. In these specific early stars, the explosion energy was simply too low to overcome", "tokens": [50365, 3931, 13, 682, 613, 2685, 2440, 6105, 11, 264, 15673, 2281, 390, 2935, 886, 2295, 281, 10473, 50683], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08504912175169778, "compression_ratio": 1.712280701754386, "no_speech_prob": 1.48925099682784e-12}, {"id": 83, "seek": 47902, "start": 485.38, "end": 490.74, "text": " the massive gravitational binding energy of the star's dense inner core. So the explosion happens,", "tokens": [50683, 264, 5994, 28538, 17359, 2281, 295, 264, 3543, 311, 18011, 7284, 4965, 13, 407, 264, 15673, 2314, 11, 50951], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08504912175169778, "compression_ratio": 1.712280701754386, "no_speech_prob": 1.48925099682784e-12}, {"id": 84, "seek": 47902, "start": 490.97999999999996, "end": 495.29999999999995, "text": " but it's not strong enough to push everything out. Right. So you get a phenomenon called gravitational", "tokens": [50963, 457, 309, 311, 406, 2068, 1547, 281, 2944, 1203, 484, 13, 1779, 13, 407, 291, 483, 257, 14029, 1219, 28538, 51179], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08504912175169778, "compression_ratio": 1.712280701754386, "no_speech_prob": 1.48925099682784e-12}, {"id": 85, "seek": 47902, "start": 495.29999999999995, "end": 501.84, "text": " fallback. The heavier, oxygen-rich inner layers of the star couldn't achieve escape velocity. They just", "tokens": [51179, 2100, 3207, 13, 440, 18279, 11, 9169, 12, 10794, 7284, 7914, 295, 264, 3543, 2809, 380, 4584, 7615, 9269, 13, 814, 445, 51506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08504912175169778, "compression_ratio": 1.712280701754386, "no_speech_prob": 1.48925099682784e-12}, {"id": 86, "seek": 47902, "start": 501.84, "end": 506.08, "text": " fell back in. They fell back inward into the central remnant, collapsing into a black hole.", "tokens": [51506, 5696, 646, 294, 13, 814, 5696, 646, 29876, 666, 264, 5777, 890, 25928, 11, 45339, 666, 257, 2211, 5458, 13, 51718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08504912175169778, "compression_ratio": 1.712280701754386, "no_speech_prob": 1.48925099682784e-12}, {"id": 87, "seek": 50608, "start": 506.08, "end": 511.47999999999996, "text": " Meanwhile, the lighter, carbon-rich outer layers, which were further from the center of gravity,", "tokens": [50365, 13879, 11, 264, 11546, 11, 5954, 12, 10794, 10847, 7914, 11, 597, 645, 3052, 490, 264, 3056, 295, 12110, 11, 50635], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08868216593331153, "compression_ratio": 1.611683848797251, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610612845787242e-12}, {"id": 88, "seek": 50608, "start": 511.91999999999996, "end": 516.24, "text": " were just barely able to escape into the surrounding gas. Oh, wow. It's quite literally", "tokens": [50657, 645, 445, 10268, 1075, 281, 7615, 666, 264, 11498, 4211, 13, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 467, 311, 1596, 3736, 50873], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08868216593331153, "compression_ratio": 1.611683848797251, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610612845787242e-12}, {"id": 89, "seek": 50608, "start": 516.24, "end": 522.34, "text": " an event log written in matter. It really is. It records a specific physical dynamical asymmetry.", "tokens": [50873, 364, 2280, 3565, 3720, 294, 1871, 13, 467, 534, 307, 13, 467, 7724, 257, 2685, 4001, 5999, 804, 37277, 9889, 13, 51178], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08868216593331153, "compression_ratio": 1.611683848797251, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610612845787242e-12}, {"id": 90, "seek": 50608, "start": 523.16, "end": 527.74, "text": " The oxygen was locked away in a black hole, refused the right to propagate into the future,", "tokens": [51219, 440, 9169, 390, 9376, 1314, 294, 257, 2211, 5458, 11, 14654, 264, 558, 281, 48256, 666, 264, 2027, 11, 51448], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08868216593331153, "compression_ratio": 1.611683848797251, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610612845787242e-12}, {"id": 91, "seek": 50608, "start": 527.98, "end": 533.64, "text": " and the carbon was allowed to escape. Yes. And because this tiny galaxy has experienced almost", "tokens": [51460, 293, 264, 5954, 390, 4350, 281, 7615, 13, 1079, 13, 400, 570, 341, 5870, 17639, 575, 6751, 1920, 51743], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08868216593331153, "compression_ratio": 1.611683848797251, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610612845787242e-12}, {"id": 92, "seek": 53364, "start": 533.64, "end": 540.16, "text": " zero subsequent star formation to stir the pot and overwrite that event, we can still read the log", "tokens": [50365, 4018, 19962, 3543, 11723, 281, 8946, 264, 1847, 293, 670, 21561, 300, 2280, 11, 321, 393, 920, 1401, 264, 3565, 50691], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.045656523861727874, "compression_ratio": 1.5405405405405406, "no_speech_prob": 1.4050223658135241e-12}, {"id": 93, "seek": 53364, "start": 540.16, "end": 547.26, "text": " 800 million years later. Yes. And that is a profound shift in perspective. The chemical abundance you", "tokens": [50691, 13083, 2459, 924, 1780, 13, 1079, 13, 400, 300, 307, 257, 14382, 5513, 294, 4585, 13, 440, 7313, 23391, 291, 51046], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.045656523861727874, "compression_ratio": 1.5405405405405406, "no_speech_prob": 1.4050223658135241e-12}, {"id": 94, "seek": 53364, "start": 547.26, "end": 551.72, "text": " measure with a telescope isn't just a list of ingredients in a cosmic recipe. Right. It's a", "tokens": [51046, 3481, 365, 257, 26114, 1943, 380, 445, 257, 1329, 295, 6952, 294, 257, 27614, 6782, 13, 1779, 13, 467, 311, 257, 51269], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.045656523861727874, "compression_ratio": 1.5405405405405406, "no_speech_prob": 1.4050223658135241e-12}, {"id": 95, "seek": 53364, "start": 551.72, "end": 557.0, "text": " history book. It's a physical record of constraints. It tells you which propagation channels were open and", "tokens": [51269, 2503, 1446, 13, 467, 311, 257, 4001, 2136, 295, 18491, 13, 467, 5112, 291, 597, 38377, 9235, 645, 1269, 293, 51533], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.045656523861727874, "compression_ratio": 1.5405405405405406, "no_speech_prob": 1.4050223658135241e-12}, {"id": 96, "seek": 55700, "start": 557.0, "end": 563.2, "text": " which were closed during the earliest moments of structure formation. It is a preserved enrichment", "tokens": [50365, 597, 645, 5395, 1830, 264, 20573, 6065, 295, 3877, 11723, 13, 467, 307, 257, 22242, 49900, 50675], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10177137496623587, "compression_ratio": 1.4814814814814814, "no_speech_prob": 1.889939593713308e-12}, {"id": 97, "seek": 55700, "start": 563.2, "end": 568.64, "text": " manifold. Okay, but here is the massive catch. Based on everything we know about telescopes and light,", "tokens": [50675, 47138, 13, 1033, 11, 457, 510, 307, 264, 5994, 3745, 13, 18785, 322, 1203, 321, 458, 466, 46051, 293, 1442, 11, 50947], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10177137496623587, "compression_ratio": 1.4814814814814814, "no_speech_prob": 1.889939593713308e-12}, {"id": 98, "seek": 55700, "start": 568.84, "end": 574.2, "text": " how did we even see LAP-1B? Yeah, that is the million-dollar question. Right. A galaxy with only", "tokens": [50957, 577, 630, 321, 754, 536, 441, 4715, 12, 16, 33, 30, 865, 11, 300, 307, 264, 2459, 12, 40485, 1168, 13, 1779, 13, 316, 17639, 365, 787, 51225], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10177137496623587, "compression_ratio": 1.4814814814814814, "no_speech_prob": 1.889939593713308e-12}, {"id": 99, "seek": 55700, "start": 574.2, "end": 580.46, "text": " 3300 solar masses of actual stars located near the dawn of time should be completely invisible to us.", "tokens": [51225, 11816, 628, 7936, 23935, 295, 3539, 6105, 6870, 2651, 264, 18192, 295, 565, 820, 312, 2584, 14603, 281, 505, 13, 51538], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10177137496623587, "compression_ratio": 1.4814814814814814, "no_speech_prob": 1.889939593713308e-12}, {"id": 100, "seek": 58046, "start": 580.46, "end": 586.3000000000001, "text": " Even for JWST, which is the most powerful eye we've ever built, it should be a ghost. It should be", "tokens": [50365, 2754, 337, 49885, 6840, 11, 597, 307, 264, 881, 4005, 3313, 321, 600, 1562, 3094, 11, 309, 820, 312, 257, 8359, 13, 467, 820, 312, 50657], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06269354496187377, 