 795.84, "text": " reality. At the bottom, we have the continuous field space, which is denoted as X. Right. Then sitting", "tokens": [51519, 4103, 13, 1711, 264, 2767, 11, 321, 362, 264, 10957, 2519, 1901, 11, 597, 307, 1441, 23325, 382, 1783, 13, 1779, 13, 1396, 3798, 51831], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.031282112711951846, "compression_ratio": 1.6286644951140066, "no_speech_prob": 1.585295696798772e-12}, {"id": 137, "seek": 79584, "start": 795.84, "end": 801.5400000000001, "text": " above that is the history-sensitive state space, denoted as H. And finally, the top layer is the", "tokens": [50365, 3673, 300, 307, 264, 2503, 12, 82, 34465, 1785, 1901, 11, 1441, 23325, 382, 389, 13, 400, 2721, 11, 264, 1192, 4583, 307, 264, 50650], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07320160718308281, "compression_ratio": 1.639676113360324, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552125559592511e-12}, {"id": 138, "seek": 79584, "start": 801.5400000000001, "end": 807.12, "text": " observable space, denoted as O, which is the stuff we actually interact with. Exactly. Let's focus on", "tokens": [50650, 9951, 712, 1901, 11, 1441, 23325, 382, 422, 11, 597, 307, 264, 1507, 321, 767, 4648, 365, 13, 7587, 13, 961, 311, 1879, 322, 50929], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07320160718308281, "compression_ratio": 1.639676113360324, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552125559592511e-12}, {"id": 139, "seek": 79584, "start": 807.12, "end": 812.32, "text": " that bottom layer. What's actually happening in the continuous fluid of reality? The continuous field", "tokens": [50929, 300, 2767, 4583, 13, 708, 311, 767, 2737, 294, 264, 10957, 9113, 295, 4103, 30, 440, 10957, 2519, 51189], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07320160718308281, "compression_ratio": 1.639676113360324, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552125559592511e-12}, {"id": 140, "seek": 79584, "start": 812.32, "end": 819.4200000000001, "text": " space X is composed of three interacting fields. It's helpful to visualize it as a fluid dynamic system.", "tokens":